  "name": "Conditional cooperation in a public goods game",
  "author": "Human Not Found",
  "description": "Original study: Fischbacher, U., Gächter, S., & Fehr, E. (2001). Are people conditionally cooperative? Evidence from a public goods experiment. Economics Letters, 71(3), 397-404. Groups of four each received 20 tokens and chose how many to put in a group project; the project was multiplied so that each token contributed returned 0.4 tokens to every member. The original measured conditional cooperation with a one-shot strategy method: each person gave an unconditional contribution and a table of contributions for every possible average contribution of the others. Public goods games are the most common paradigm in our survey of 500 multi-participant experiments (65 studies). This template runs the same group size, endowment and return over 10 repeated rounds instead of the strategy method: after each round everyone sees every member's contribution and their own earnings, so conditional cooperation shows as contributions that follow the others' previous contributions. Participants are simulated: each is a Model participant, using the free test model until you choose a model, so the first runs only check the design; with the test model every participant takes the sample answer.",
